ine from that  68k  pop down to about 64. now that's a small  change it's not totally obvious that  we're on the way back down but at least  we're not continuing to see an increase  and if vaccination continues to  accelerate we should see  substantial case declines over the next  eight weeks  and i'll tell you why a little bit later  in terms of deaths  the strategy of vaccinating the most  likely to die  if they get the virus after doing health  care workers of course  is working even as cases leveled out and  we said well  you know deaths are on a three-week  delay from cases we may see deaths level  out  they didn't deaths have continued down  the entire time  you might remember they peaked at nearly  3 500  per day more than a 911 a day in january  they went below 3 000 a day in the first  week of february  below 2 000 a day around february 20th  below 1 000  a day uh in that fourth week of march  and now  down to just a hair over 800 deaths a  day  still horrifying still a tragic number  but one that is now down about 77  from its peak in late january this  should continue because cases aside  even against the variants the vaccines  are nearly 100  effective at preventing death  vaccination  over the weekend the u.s had consecutive  days  with 4 million vaccines per day we are  now  averaging just over 3 million doses  per day this means that every day  roughly one percent of the u.s  population is getting a shot  now it's not one percent is being fully  vaccinated each day a lot of those are  two dose regimens  so still one percent of the us  population is now getting  a shot every day the reason we know this  will pay off  in about six to eight weeks is we look  at israeli data  israel has now vaccinated more than 55  percent  of its population they are basically  wide open  passover was almost a completely normal  passover with gatherings and everything  and cases and hospitalizations  serious coveted deaths are all down  down down dramatically we have the 